/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - An active transducer makes its own signal (a thermocouple, a piezoelectric crystal); a passive one changes a resistance, capacitance or inductance and needs an excitation supply (a strain gauge, an RTD, an LVDT).
   - A solenoid is a coil wound round a movable iron plunger.
   - F is the pull on the plunger face in N; B is the flux density in the air gap in T; A is the pole-face area in m²; μ₀ = 4π × 10⁻⁷ H/m.
   - Pressure is force per area, in pascals (1 bar = 100 kPa).
   - A resistance temperature detector (RTD) is a platinum wire or film whose resistance rises almost linearly with temperature: RT = R₀(1 + αT), with R₀ = 100 Ω at 0 °C and α = 0.00385 /°C for the common Pt100.
   - A thermocouple is two different metals joined at the hot end.
   - GF is the gauge factor (about 2 for metal foil); ΔR is the change of resistance and R the unstrained resistance in Ω; ε = ΔL ÷ L is the strain (1 µε = 10⁻⁶).
   - A strain gauge is a foil grid bonded to a part; when the part stretches, the grid lengthens and its resistance rises.
   - Answer. ΔR = 0.70 Ω; full-scale output 20 mV; 0.20 mV/kPa, so 7.0 mV at 35 kPa; a quarter bridge would give only about 5.0 mV.
*/
