/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A diode conducts from anode to cathode once the forward voltage is about 0.7 V (silicon) and blocks the other way.
   - A bipolar junction transistor (BJT) has base, collector and emitter.
   - An operational amplifier is a very high-gain differential amplifier.
   - A is the voltage gain; Rf is the feedback resistor from the output to the inverting input; Rin goes from the inverting input to the signal (inverting) or to ground (non-inverting).
   - A differential amplifier uses one op-amp and four resistors; with R₁ = R₃ and R₂ = R₄ it amplifies the difference of its inputs by R₂ ÷ R₁ and rejects the voltage common to both.
   - Signal conditioning then means: amplify to fill the ADC range; filter (an anti-alias low-pass filter removes noise above half the sampling rate; a first-order RC filter has cut-off fc = 1 ÷ (2πRC)); isolate where grounds differ; clamp the i
   - Q is the resolution (one step) in volts; Vref is the reference voltage; N is the number of bits; Vin is the input; floor rounds down.
   - Converter types: successive approximation (SAR), the usual general-purpose type, tries one bit at a time from the most significant, so an N-bit conversion takes N steps; flash uses 2N − 1 comparators for the fastest conversion; sigma-delta 
   - A DAC does the reverse: Vout = k × Vref ÷ 2N for code k.
   - A microprocessor is a CPU on a chip; memory and input/output are separate chips.
*/
